.", "newValue": "Niccolò Gaddi (1499–1552) was an Italian Roman Catholic cardinal. A prominent figure in the Counter-Reformation, he played a crucial role in shaping the Catholic Church's response to Protestantism during the 16th century. As a member of the Council of Trent, Gaddi contributed to the development of the Tridentine Constitution, which solidified Catholic doctrine and practices. His involvement with the Holy Roman Emperor Charles V further underscored his significance as a key advisor to European monarchs.

Gaddi's legacy extends beyond his theological contributions, as he was also a skilled diplomat and strategist who navigated the complex web of alliances between European powers. This expertise allowed him to effectively promote Catholic interests and secure support for the Church during a tumultuous period in European history.

